Clear a cache buffer before handing it out for a block nobody read

ext4_block_get_noread returns a buffer without reading anything into it -- the caller is
expected to fill it. But the buffer either came straight from malloc, or was last used for a
different block. Whatever it holds reaches the disk in every byte the caller does not
overwrite.

In a firmware image built on a machine with address randomisation, that shows up as heap
addresses sitting in the padding after a directory entry, different on every build.

A handful of bytes in a gigabyte. Two people cannot then confirm they built the same
firmware, and fragments of the building process travel inside a file that gets published.

--- WHY HERE AND NOT AT THE CALL SITE ---

lwext4 has an unwritten contract: whoever calls _get_noread clears the buffer. Most callers
do. Of the twenty-eight call sites, these do not:

    ext4_dir_idx.c    427, 976        directory index
    ext4_extent.c    1036, 1376       extent tree
    ext4_mkfs.c       325
    ext4_journal.c    eight places

Clearing it in ext4_dir_idx.c alone is not enough, because the extent tree leaks too. Doing
it where the buffer is handed out covers all of them.

--- WHY ONLY WHEN is_new ---

If the buffer is already cached for this same block it may be holding writes that have not
reached the disk yet. Clearing that would throw them away -- trading a quiet leak for a
loud loss.

--- WHY NO TOOL CATCHES IT ---

valgrind names the ext4_dir_idx.c sites and no more: it sees uninitialised memory, and a
buffer recycled from another block is initialised, just with somebody else's data. That
half is invisible to it, so the leak has to be reasoned about rather than hunted. The
plainest symptom is a directory created inside a directory: the same change made twice on
a pristine image gives two different images.

With the clear in place, two builds from identical input produce the same file, byte for
byte.

--- WHY NOT THE ALTERNATIVES ---

CONFIG_USE_USER_MALLOC, lwext4's own hook for supplying an allocator, would avoid touching
the source. It segfaults: the header defines the macro but never declares the prototypes,
so the call is made without one and the pointer is truncated to 32 bits. That is a second
bug in its own right.

Interposing our own ext4_block_get_noread at build time cannot work either: the wrapper
cannot see is_new, and without it the clear becomes destructive.

--- a/src/ext4_blockdev.c
+++ b/src/ext4_blockdev.c
@@
 	if (!b->data)
 		return ENOMEM;
 
+	if (is_new)
+		memset(b->data, 0, bdev->bc->itemsize);
+
 	return EOK;
 }
